    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dd30859a-fdb1-5c1c-991a-c79d100877ee",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & - \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{20}- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(- \\frac{1}{4}- \\frac{3}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{15}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4})+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{4})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{15} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
