    "question": {
      ":question/id": "eb644a1f-27b6-5ed8-948f-77f04d00ec88",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & -2 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & -2 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ -2 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}- \\sqrt{5})+(-1)(\\frac{5}{8}- \\frac{15}{4})+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\cdot (- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4})+(-1)\\cdot (- \\frac{25}{8})+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{75}{16}+\\frac{25}{8}+\\frac{25}{16} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
