      ":question/id": "ac68321d-566d-51d3-9027-74b9e95c777b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=-42$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & -1 & - 2 \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & -1 & - 2 \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ -1 & - 2 \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & - 2 \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{7}}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}(- 3 \\sqrt{7}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-14+\\frac{56}{9})+(- \\sqrt{7})(- \\sqrt{7}- 2 \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{7}}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{70}{9})+(- \\sqrt{7})\\cdot (- 3 \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{154}{9}- \\frac{35}{9}+21 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ee9ad6b8-6c6c-564d-ba3f-871398b78843",
