  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "138aa783-d25a-55bb-a2b8-9c3752526774",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=5$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2}{5}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+2 \\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5})+\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{10}{3})+2 \\sqrt{5}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{15}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\cdot 0+\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{25}{6}+2 \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=5\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "190351a8-2a1d-560f-b8e5-bc1f155b59c1",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
