    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b2d15a44-cdd7-5964-9a77-b03e3c78e474",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=- \\sqrt{5}$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{4}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{15})+(- \\frac{4}{5})(- \\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{5}{3})+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{15}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{5}}{15}+(- \\frac{4}{5})\\cdot 0+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})\\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}- \\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
