  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d105261-5a11-5c58-839e-0bfcbf02d1c2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=3$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4})+(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})(- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{8}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{8}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})\\cdot \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6b67ec06-3e37-52e0-87a4-cbedc4917d67",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
