    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7381eae7-c18b-594f-a819-a6bb90d53917",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(A)=\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & 1 & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & 1 & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{3}{2})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & 1 \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3})+(- \\frac{3}{2})(\\frac{16}{3}- \\frac{16}{3})+\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}+(- \\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
