  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "930c1c68-6f14-5f9f-b4dd-0f66419648ce",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=\\sqrt{2}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{2})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & -1 \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(- \\frac{1}{2})(- \\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})+(- \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "73314d7d-6bb0-553f-a22e-9f86bdee6ef4",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
