  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "522fca49-e4f2-5962-a41a-9accbba3a35c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=\\frac{27}{4}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & - \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & 2 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & - \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & 2 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5}|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & 2 \\\\ - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8})+\\frac{1}{2}(3+\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{11 \\sqrt{5}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=\\frac{41}{4}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ea0bf135-a2b2-53d4-8c55-692e9a59830c",
