    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a14b653d-a2e7-5513-bb30-755008357f06",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=0$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5})(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{9}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\frac{6 \\sqrt{3}}{25}- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{25}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{5})\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}- \\frac{18}{25}- \\frac{9}{50} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "48ac11eb-3b9c-5099-adb7-2266d2488157",
