  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1f5a2750-b734-5a1a-a9d5-a93410eeb42c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=\\frac{112}{9}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ - 4 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{3} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ - 4 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{3} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{3} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ - 4 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & -1 \\\\ - 4 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{3}+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{9})+(- \\frac{1}{3})(- \\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{28}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}- 4 \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{8 \\sqrt{7}}{9}+(- \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 7+\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{13 \\sqrt{7}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{9}- \\frac{7}{3}- \\frac{91}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
