  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3ac42bf4-38d4-5643-bb78-2c57307da775",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=0$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}- \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}- \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{20}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(- \\frac{15}{4}- \\frac{15}{8})+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{5}}{20}+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot (- \\frac{45}{8})+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\cdot (- \\frac{13 \\sqrt{5}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}+\\frac{9}{4}- \\frac{39}{8} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
